  "title": "Operational K9 Airway Management",
  "markdown": "# Operational K9 Airway Management\n\n(continued from previous page)\n\n> \\*Pulse oximetry is most reliable in unconscious, sedated, or anesthetized canines. Finger probes used for people do not work well in canines. If possible, obtain and use a flat ear probe attachment. Place the probe on the tongue or non-pigmented portion of the lip. In conscious dogs, use the ear pinna, lip fold, inguinal skin fold or prepuce/vulva; although not optimal for oximetry, these alternate sites generally yield reliable results in most instances. Alternatively , a neonatal or disposable pulse oximetry adhesive sensor attached to the base of the canine's tail provides an alternative and very reliable site.\n",
  "plain_text": "Operational K9 Airway Management (continued from previous page) Pulse oximetry is most reliable in unconscious, sedated, or anesthetized canines. Finger probes used for people do not work well in canines. If possible, obtain and use a flat ear probe attachment. Place the probe on the tongue or non-pigmented portion of the lip. In conscious dogs, use the ear pinna, lip fold, inguinal skin fold or prepuce/vulva; although not optimal for oximetry, these alternate sites generally yield reliable results in most instances. Alternatively , a neonatal or disposable pulse oximetry adhesive sensor attached to the base of the canine's tail provides an alternative and very reliable site.",
